<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horus was an ancient Egyptian sky god, shown as a falcon or a falcon-headed man. In myth he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> who avenged his father against Set, and every living king was seen as Horus on earth, using a Horus name written in a serekh. His early cult centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a>, and he was later worshipped widely, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horus.jpg" alt="Horus" width="320" height="732" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horus_standing.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Horus was worshipped from at least late prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> and Roman Egypt. His name was written in hieroglyphs as ḥr.w, meaning 'falcon'. The earliest recorded form is the tutelary deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Nekhen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, the first known national god, bound to the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. Egyptian tradition and archaeological evidence indicate that an Upper Egyptian kingdom conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> at the beginning of Egyptian history. Its rulers called themselves followers of Horus, and Horus became the tutelary deity of the united state and its kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> was identified directly with Horus, who represented kingship itself. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a>, of about 2400 to 2300 BCE, describe the king as both Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>: Horus in life and Osiris in death. In the most common version of the myth Horus is the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> and Osiris. After Set killed Osiris, Isis fled to the marshes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and bore Horus there. He fought many battles with Set to avenge his father and decide the rightful ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In The Contendings of Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> the gods eventually sided with Horus, and Set gave up the throne.</p><p>Horus was held to be the sky, with the sun as his right eye and the moon as his left. The Eye of Horus, the wedjat, was a symbol of protection and royal power, and funerary amulets were often made in its shape. Horus appeared as a falcon or as a man with a falcon's head.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Horus had many forms. Heru-ur, Horus the Elder, was worshipped at Letopolis and later at Kom Ombo and Qus, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. Heru-pa-khered, Horus the child, whom the Greeks called Harpocrates, is shown as a youth with a lock of hair, sucking his finger. Horus of Behdet was the winged sun, depicted above the pylons of temples throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. At the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> an annual Festival of Victory commemorated the victory of Horus over Set: the king played Horus and struck a hippopotamus, standing for Set, with a harpoon, an act that also legitimised the king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Horus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…throne at Thebes around 2055 BCE, fought the Herakleopolitans and their allies in Middle Egypt and, probably within the first four decades of a reign of about fifty years, reunited the country; he marked the achievement by taking the <b>Horus</b> name Sematawy, 'he who unites the Two Lands'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptians believed the king to be the incarnation of <b>Horus</b>, linking the human and spiritual worlds, and saw the universe as working in cycles that the king on earth laboured to keep stable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Each district of Egypt had its own gods, often in animal form: the falcon <b>Horus</b>, the jackal Anubis, the cow Hathor, the crocodile Sobek, the ram Khnum.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Frank Yurco noted that royal crowns, <b>Horus</b> falcons and victory scenes were concentrated in Naqada Upper Egypt and the A-Group of Lower Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesopotamian kings governed as servants of their cities’ gods, and the Egyptian pharaoh was identified with the god <b>Horus</b> and mediated between gods and people.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the New Kingdom it was revered as the solar god Hor-em-akhet, <b>Horus</b> of the Horizon, called Harmachis by the Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-sphinx-of-giza">Great Sphinx of Giza · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
